---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CommunityLinks from '../components/CommunityLinks.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="NoRec.US | Government surveillance, documented from the source"
  description="NoRec.US documents government surveillance systems, public records, laws, policies, and the evidence behind the claims."
>
  <main class="home_shell">
    <section class="home_hero">
      <div class="home_hero_copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">ILLINOIS · PRIVACY · PRIMARY SOURCES</p>
        <h1>Do you have an Illinois ID?</h1>
        <p class="qr_lede">If you have an Illinois driver’s license, permit, or state ID, the Secretary of State maintains a photograph captured through that credential process. Illinois law also expressly addresses facial-recognition search services involving those photographs.</p>
        <p class="qr_note"><strong>The credential population is not adult-only.</strong> Illinois says there is no minimum age for a state ID card, and instruction permits may be issued beginning at age 15 under specified conditions.</p>
        <div class="hero_actions">
          <a class="button button_dark button_big" href="/surveillance/face/illinois-id-photos/">See the Illinois evidence →</a>
          <a class="button button_light" href="/sources/">Open the source library</a>
        </div>
        <p class="hero_caution">NoRec does not claim that every credential photo is stored as a persistent biometric template. Where the records do not establish something, we mark it <strong>UNKNOWN</strong>.</p>
      </div>

      <aside class="home_receipt">
        <p class="eyebrow">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
        <div class="receipt_row"><span>DOCUMENTED</span><strong>Illinois maintains captured credential photographs.</strong></div>
        <div class="receipt_row"><span>DOCUMENTED</span><strong>State law expressly addresses facial-recognition search services.</strong></div>
        <div class="receipt_row"><span>DOCUMENTED</span><strong>The credential population includes minors.</strong></div>
        <div class="receipt_row receipt_unknown"><span>UNKNOWN</span><strong>Exact historical retention, template architecture, and total search volume.</strong></div>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="home_rule">
      <p class="eyebrow">THE RULE</p>
      <h2>If the evidence doesn't establish it, we don't present it as fact.</h2>
      <p>NoRec.US is a privacy and public-records project documenting government surveillance technology and the systems that connect it. The site has a point of view. The evidentiary standard does not.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="home_grid">
      <a class="home_card home_card_feature" href="/surveillance/face/illinois-id-photos/">
        <span class="card_kicker">FACIAL RECOGNITION</span>
        <h2>Illinois ID photos</h2>
        <p>Illinois law, age scope, competing arguments, and the questions the available record does not answer.</p>
        <strong>Read the investigation →</strong>
      </a>
      <a class="home_card" href="/illinois/">
        <span class="card_kicker">LOCAL SURVEILLANCE</span>
        <h2>Illinois county map</h2>
        <p>Browse documented systems by county, or contribute records where coverage is still being built.</p>
        <strong>Open the map →</strong>
      </a>
      <a class="home_card" href="/sources/">
        <span class="card_kicker">PRIMARY SOURCES</span>
        <h2>Source library</h2>
        <p>Statutes, regulations, government reports, policies, contracts, FOIA responses, and archived evidence.</p>
        <strong>View the sources →</strong>
      </a>
      <a class="home_card" href="/research/">
        <span class="card_kicker">OPEN QUESTIONS</span>
        <h2>Research & FOIA</h2>
        <p>See what is documented, what remains unknown, and the records needed to answer it.</p>
        <strong>See active research →</strong>
      </a>

      <a class="home_card" href="/why-privacy-matters/">
        <span class="card_kicker">WHY CARE?</span>
        <h2>Why privacy matters</h2>
        <p>The case for privacy even if the agency is trustworthy, the technology is accurate, and you have nothing illegal to hide.</p>
        <strong>Read the argument →</strong>
      </a>

      <a class="home_card" href="/about/">
        <span class="card_kicker">WHY NOREC</span>
        <h2>About the project</h2>
        <p>From ALPR concerns to data fusion, biometric systems, and a broader effort to make surveillance infrastructure visible.</p>
        <strong>Why this exists →</strong>
      </a>
    </section>

    <section class="home_community">
      <div class="home_community_copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">COMMUNITY</p>
        <h2>Help document what is happening locally.</h2>
        <p>Found a contract, policy, board packet, camera inventory, FOIA response, or surveillance lead? Contribute the record on GitHub or share the lead with the group.</p>
      </div>
      <CommunityLinks />
    </section>

    <section class="home_scope">
      <p class="eyebrow">SCOPE</p>
      <h2>Not just cameras.</h2>
      <div class="scope_tags">
        <span>Facial recognition</span>
        <span>ALPR / vehicle tracking</span>
        <span>Data fusion</span>
        <span>Surveillance cameras</span>
        <span>Cell-site simulators</span>
        <span>Government databases</span>
        <span>Vendor systems</span>
        <span>FOIA records</span>
      </div>
      <p>The project follows the data: what is collected, how it is searched, where it travels, how long it remains, who can access it, and what changes when separate systems become one network.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="home_privacy">
      <p class="eyebrow">THIS SITE PRACTICES WHAT IT PREACHES</p>
      <h2>No ads. No tracking pixels. No third-party fonts. No social embeds.</h2>
      <p>The site is static and lightweight. Reading about privacy should not create another unnecessary trail of visitor data.</p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
